\section{Column widths}{

\code{column_widths_twips} is absolute, so a subset already carries the right
widths and \code{width} has no effect. Relative widths (and the default equal
distribution) need a rule, because \code{.compute_cellx()} re-normalises whatever
it is given across the page: a bare subset would stretch the kept columns to
refill the sheet, making a ratio-1 column a different size on every page.

\code{width = "fill"} (the default) fixes the \strong{twips per ratio unit on page 1}
and reuses it everywhere. Page 1 -- and any block with the same ratio total
-- fills the sheet, while a given ratio is the same width on every page.
With \code{rel = c(3, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1)} on a 13680-twip page:\tabular{lll}{
   blocks \tab unit \tab page widths \cr
   4 + 4 \tab 1954.3 \tab 100\% / 100\% \cr
   4 + 3 \tab 1954.3 \tab 100\% / 85.7\% \cr
   2+2+2+2 \tab 2736.0 \tab 100\% on all four \cr
}


\code{width = "keep"} measures against the whole table instead, so a kept column
has exactly the width it had before the split and a partial block yields a
proportionally shorter page.

A block totalling \strong{more} ratio than page 1 scales past the sheet under
\code{"fill"}; a warning names the pages. Order the blocks so the widest comes
first, or use \code{"keep"}.
}

\section{Spanning headers}{

Spanning cells are clipped to each page's columns. By default a cut may
fall inside a spanning group, and the group's label is repeated over its
remaining columns on each page; \code{allow_span_break = FALSE} rejects such a
cut instead.
}

\section{Page names}{

A column page inherits the name of the row page it was cut from, whatever
\code{page_order} is -- that is the only name there is. Under \code{"down"} pages that
share a name are therefore no longer adjacent.

What that does \emph{not} change is the sectioning:
\code{rtf_tables(auto_section = TRUE)} opens a section at every \strong{named} page,
and a page whose name is \code{""} joins the section before it. Equal names are
\strong{not} merged, so a named page list gives a section per page under either
order, and an \strong{unnamed} list -- what \code{\link[=as_rtftables]{as_rtftables()}} returns for a single
table, and what \code{paginate_cols()} then passes through -- gives none at all,
leaving every page in the document's own section.

To put a run of pages in one section, name its first page and blank the
rest -- \code{\link[=combine_sections]{combine_sections()}} does that bookkeeping when you are assembling
whole tables, and when the names come from the pages themselves (a
\code{page_by} / \code{"by_value"} run) the same thing is two lines:

